g the command-level evidence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-67be658b939e4c70","cmd-4adaa5e8440841de","cmd-a5a8682ac679494a","cmd-095f0e494fb84a69","cmd-9d70d0833ff845e4","cmd-d2b6ee76763b44bf","cmd-d7e6455a1c5749a2","cmd-c6f4bb4bb5a549b8","cmd-bb3511c6e75f4da2","cmd-d55003936ac643da","cmd-32d44f9d03604617","cmd-04ccd50780eb45fa","cmd-dd063c07ce8e4121","cmd-1c5ac81624554790","cmd-df8fff3831d04c7a","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.7,"summary":"Most mutations were purposeful and either bounded/reverted or retained as necessary supporting infrastructure. However, SSH key material (a private key at ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 plus an authorized_keys entry) was installed on all three nodes (cmd-1141705e4a1e4e42, cmd-99e4d6962a4b465f, cmd-c369b654051243ec) to enable scp-based password transfer; the scp attempt then failed because sshd was inactive on the targets (cmd-f022048d34dd4df4, cmd-171197cdc1594e49), and the executor pivoted to an ad hoc HTTP transfer instead. The SSH keys were never used successfully and were never removed, leaving unused private-key residue on all three managed nodes with no evidence-supported role in the final outcome. The subsequent ad hoc HTTP server used to transfer the replication password (cmd-4aee9880ed014f90) was bounded, low-risk, and was properly torn down (process killed and temp file removed) via cmd-e02ff90a3c634f34 and cmd-f3d377200b8a422d. The temporary xfer directory was also cleaned up at the end (cmd-c3fda525dfbe4139). No failed services, unsafe broad exposure, or damaged package state were observed in the after-executor snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1141705e4a1e4e42","cmd-99e4d6962a4b465f","cmd-c369b654051243ec","cmd-f022048d34dd4df4","cmd-171197cdc1594e49","cmd-4aee9880ed014f90","cmd-e02ff90a3c634f34","cmd-f3d377200b8a422d","cmd-c3fda525dfbe4139","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor established genuine PostgreSQL 16 streaming replication (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys), then directly demonstrated a full failover cycle: stopped node1, confirmed node2 already held the last committed row before promoting it via pg_promote(), re-pointed node3 to the new primary, and rebuilt node1 as a standby of node2 — all confirmed with direct queries rather than just configuration. All three nodes were also subjected to the mandatory one-at-a-time reboot protocol with clean boot-id transitions and confirmed data/service recovery, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The one notable blemish is unused SSH key material left on all three nodes from an abandoned password-transfer approach that was superseded by an HTTP-based transfer.","limitations":["The evidence trace does not show whether the private SSH key/authorized_keys residue was later removed after the observed after-executor snapshot; it appears to remain in place.","Password/credential contents were redacted throughout the trace, so their exact values could not be independently verified, though behavior (successful basebackups and streaming) confirms they were functional."]},"ttft_ms":19433,"type":"result","duration_ms":79764,"uuid":"ff282120-d0fa-4739-8a78-c9a2ff2f80f5","ttft_stream_ms":2312,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":2913,"queued_turn_count":0}
